[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe problem:[22m The test runner loads the compiled Rust shared library ([38;5;10mlibsha2.so[0m) and looks up the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_initialize_hash_function[0m via [38;5;10mdlsym[0m. In the original C code, the function name is mangled by a preprocessor macro:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10m#define initialize_hash_function SPX_NAMESPACE(initialize_hash_function)
// expands to: SPX_initialize_hash_function
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation had a correct internal [38;5;10minitialize_hash_function()[0m in [38;5;10mhash.rs[0m, and [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m exported many other functions with [38;5;10m#[no_mangle] extern "C"[0m (like [38;5;10mcrypto_sign_keypair[0m, [38;5;10mcrypto_sign[0m, etc.), but it was missing the [38;5;10mSPX_initialize_hash_function[0m export that the test harness expected.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe fix:[22m Added a single exported wrapper function to [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub extern "C" fn SPX_initialize_hash_function(ctx: *mut SpxCtx) {
    let ctx = unsafe { &mut *ctx };
    initialize_hash_function(ctx);
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This makes the symbol visible in the shared library with the exact name the test runner expects. The underlying SHA-256 implementation and [38;5;10mseed_state[0m logic were already correct — the only issue was the missing FFI export.[0m[0m
